{"action": "diagnose", "why": "The results support the report's chain: provider 500s and timeouts tripped the payments-api breaker, and the breaker now answers 503 to almost every POST /payments. No deploy changed anything, so no listed action fits.", "diagnosis": {"hypothesis": "From about 20:56, fake-provider (outside our control) began answering about 11-14% of payments-api's POST /v1/payments with 500 and letting some calls hang until our 3 s read timeout. That tripped payments-api's circuit breaker (PROVIDER_BREAKER=on) between 20:56:25 and 20:56:55. While open, the breaker rejects calls without sending them, leaves each payment pending and answers 503 at about 10/s, while 201s fall to 0. Each half-open trial call, about every 10-13 s, again hangs for 3 s and times out, so the breaker stays open. This gives the 5xx share. The slow requests behind the p95 are the provider timeouts, from the onset and from the trials. The cause is the provider; the breaker works as designed and turns provider trouble into fast 503s. Left open: why the provider fails; the breaker's thresholds; and why some requests exceed one timeout (the 6.2 s trace, and the 3.7 s p95 above the 3 s timeout).", "evidence": [{"call_id": "c1", "shows": "payments-api 503s rise from 0.15/s at 20:56:25 to 4.82/s at 20:56:55 and 10.02/s at 20:57:25. 201s fall from 3.72/s to 0. 200s appear from 20:56:55, which is unexplained and possibly idempotent replays."}, {"call_id": "c2", "shows": "Every recent ERROR reads 'provider unavailable; payment left pending' with '/v1/payments not sent: the circuit breaker is open (the next trial call in N s)'. Some payment_ids repeat (pmt_000397, 000396, 000395, 000394), so clients are retrying into the open breaker."}, {"call_id": "c4", "shows": "Provider client calls: 500s at 0.36/s (20:56:05) and 0.48/s (20:56:35) against about 3/s of 200s, then both fall to 0 by 20:57:35 as the breaker stops sending. No ReadTimeout series appears, so this metric does not record the timeouts."}, {"call_id": "c6", "shows": "The p95 of provider calls that got an answer stays at 5-8 ms, so the slow requests are the unanswered (timed-out) calls, which this histogram does not record."}, {"call_id": "c7", "shows": "Slow POST /payments traces of 3005-3012 ms at 20:56:38, 20:56:48, 20:57:02 and 20:57:15, about 10-13 s apart, consistent with breaker trial calls timing out. One 6224 ms trace at 20:56:35 was not examined. The result is truncated (more_may_exist)."}, {"call_id": "c8", "shows": "pmt_000363 at 20:57:15: 3010 ms, ended in 503. 3006 ms is in POST http://fake-provider:8000/v1/payments, which ended in httpx.ReadTimeout. The Redis and Postgres spans take under 1 ms, which rules them out."}, {"call_id": "c5", "shows": "All containers were created at 20:55:01-02 (a fresh start). No deploy or settings change came between the start and the failures at about 20:56, so no deploy is implicated. payments-api runs PROVIDER_BREAKER=on."}, {"call_id": "c3", "shows": "klaxon_provider_attempts_total has no outcome label (a single unlabelled series), so it gives no breakdown by outcome. It still shows 1.04/s at 20:57:31 while c4 shows 0 sent calls, a mismatch left unexplained."}], "confidence": 0.65, "proposed_fix": {"kind": "none", "target": "fake-provider (external) / payments-api circuit breaker", "summary": "There is no change to make on our side: the fault is the provider's. Keep the breaker on; turning it off would send every request into 3 s timeouts and more possible unknown charges. Watch for trial calls to succeed and the breaker to close. Afterwards, run the money check, and reconcile payments left pending and calls that timed out against the provider's records and webhooks. A timed-out call may have charged at the provider; payment-confirmer should mark those succeeded when their webhooks arrive."}, "blast_radius": "No change is made. Payments keep failing with 503 and stay pending until the provider recovers. Calls that timed out may have charged at the provider without our knowing until their webhooks or the money check reveal them.", "rollback_plan": "Nothing to undo, since no change is made. If the provider recovers but the breaker does not close, escalate to a human to inspect the breaker's settings."}}
```

